Byte comparison (4096-byte source regions):
  src[0..63]  = 64 x 0x41 for poc.exr; 64 x 0x5a for poc5a.exr (in-bounds, file pixels).
  src[64..4095] vs same-input repeat: differ at 8 offsets only: 3448..3455.
  src[64..4095] vs pixel-variant:     differ at the same 8 offsets only: 3448..3455.
  Example 3448..3455: 39 90 f8 7d 05 b9 63 a1 (run 1),
                      32 f7 c7 0a b4 7a ff 6b,
                      pixel-5a = 12 fd 63 4d 56 19 91 aa.
  Histogram of src[64..4095] run 1: 3528 zero bytes, 201 x 0x55, rest heap metadata
  and parsed-header residue; 0x41 occurs 3 times, none a contiguous file string.
  16-byte runs of poc.exr found in src[64..] are zero/header-field coincidences;
  no 24-byte or 32-byte run of the file occurs there.

Conclusion: in the real allocator the copied OOB bytes are allocator/heap
metadata, not attacker-chosen file bytes. The only run-varying 8-byte window
(3448..3455) varies on a same-input repeat, so it is not file-controlled.
Structural ceiling from W163/W164 holds on the aborting plain build too:
the file sets the copy length (4096) and destination offset, not OOB contents.

